hen both endpoints are finite.","When no limit is used."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"629e87a7bb887ce3e67d6ac9"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What happens to $\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["It converges.","It diverges.","It is not an improper integral.","It equals $0$ by definition."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca5ebefbe5bfb3902b39f94b"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What happens to $\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["It converges.","It is a finite geometric series.","It is an indefinite integral.","It diverges."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"608d7877386cbdc8d9085c0a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are limits used in improper integrals?","options":["They remove the integrand from the integral.","They make every integral convergent.","They turn every integral into a finite sum.","They formalise accumulation for unbounded cases"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b0b9c2815f611646bcc7c962"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which situation can make an integral improper?","options":["The integrand is a polynomial on a closed bounded interval.","The variable is named $x$.","The integral has a numerical value.","An endpoint of integration approaches $+\\infty$ or $-\\infty$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8f37043653fa0d7dccd1cb53"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which limit expression matches $\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\lim_{b\\to 1}\\int_1^b\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{b\\to+\\infty}\\int_1^b\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^1\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{b\\to+\\infty}\\frac{1}{b^2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"56e94e32d4937878fcaaf1ba"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which integral is improper because the upper endpoint is infinite?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^\\infty\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3fa60d0543bc0dea35289651"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes convergence from being improper?","options":["Every improper integral converges.","It may converge or diverge, per its limit","Every improper integral diverges.","Improper integrals do not use limits."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bce463bf86e5f584abce293f"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Besides infinite endpoints, what other endpoint behaviour can make an integral improper?","options":["The integrand becomes unbounded at one or both endpoints.","The integrand is continuous at all endpoints.","The interval is closed and bounded.","The integrand has a constant value."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7f9b22dd900a577a01a10a46"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which integral is improper because the integrand is unbounded at an endpoint?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^1 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"db113c6ae77213d8569f525f"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_improper_integral_c2373328_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"What should be concluded if the defining limit of an improper integral does not exist?","options":["The improper integral is convergent.","The integral becomes an indefinite integral.","The improper integral is divergent.","The integrand must be zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"753e7c86825a52193b2a5974"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which feature can make an integral improper?","options":["An infinite endpoint of integration.","A finite closed interval with a bounded integrand.","Changing the dummy variable name.","The integrand being equal to $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"48eaa6bb26c03f4a6e84a850"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When is an improper integral convergent?","options":["When the upper endpoint is always finite.","When its defining limit exists.","When no limit is used.","When the integrand is named $x$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dc4454608d22427c991f5cd6"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How are improper integrals defined?","options":["As limits of definite integrals.","As derivatives at endpoints.","As finite products.","As unordered sets."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7b274537547b607bc47614f5"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which integral is improper because of an infinite endpoint?","options":["$\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_2^{+\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_{-1}^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7d590046db288b72043725a1"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which integral is improper due to a singularity at an endpoint?","options":["$\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_1^2 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_2^3 x^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d3db2ce96ed6c24bbecc7e84"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which limit expression defines $\\int_0^{+\\infty} f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\lim_{b\\to0}\\int_0^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_0^0 f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\lim_{b\\to+\\infty} f(b)$","$\\lim_{b\\to+\\infty}\\int_0^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eb85942ea6f0a71d9d861d3c"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is being improper different from being divergent?","options":["Improper is the setup; divergence means no limiting value","Every improper integral diverges by definition.","Every improper integral converges by definition.","Improper integrals do not use limits."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eece062f5eb2058c6f6b5b2a"},{"id":"improper_integral_expansion_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression tests convergence of $\\int_1^{+\\infty}\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\lim_{b\\to1}\\int_1^b\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\int_1^1\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\lim_{b\\to+\\infty}\\int_1^b\\frac{1}{x^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1}{x^2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"350419fc59575334f0638d1b"}]}}